{"name":"Black Iris","hex":"#2b3042","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"black","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Absorbing all light and reflecting none; dark and hueless.","Without light."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour/color perceived in the absence of light, but also when no light is reflected, but rather absorbed.","A black dye or pigment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/black"},{"term":"iris","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of the genus Iris, common in the northern hemisphere, and generally having attractive blooms (See  Iris (plant) on  Wikipedia.Wikipedia ).","The contractile membrane perforated by the pupil, which adjusts to control the amount of light reaching the retina, and which forms the colored portion of the eye (See  Iris (anatomy) on  Wikipedia.Wikipedia )."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To open or close in the manner of an iris.","To cause (something) to shine with the colours of the rainbow; to make iridescent."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/iris"}],"entries":[{"title":"Black Iris (painting)","blocks":[{"text":"Black Iris, formerly called Black Iris III, is a 1926 oil painting by Georgia O'Keeffe. Art historian Linda Nochlin interpreted Black Iris as a morphological metaphor for female genitalia. O'Keeffe rejected such interpretations in a 1939 text accompanying an exhibition of her work, in which she wrote: \"Well—I made you take time to look at what I saw and when you took time to really notice my flower you hung all your own associations with flowers on my flower and you write about my flower as if I think and see what you think and see of the flower—and I don't.\" She attempted to do away with sexualized readings of her work by adding a lot of detail."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Georgia_O%27Keeffe_-_Black_Iris_1926_MET.jpg/330px-Georgia_O%27Keeffe_-_Black_Iris_1926_MET.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Iris_(painting)"},{"title":"Iris chrysographes","blocks":[{"text":"Iris chrysographes, the black iris, is a plant species that belongs to the genus Iris. It is native to Southern China and Myanmar (Burma), growing in meadows, streamsides, hillsides and forest margins."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Iris_chrysographes_138-8433.jpg/330px-Iris_chrysographes_138-8433.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iris_chrysographes"},{"title":"Iris (anatomy)","blocks":[{"text":"The iris is a thin, ring-shaped structure in the eye in most mammals and birds that is responsible for controlling the diameter and size of the pupil, and thus the amount of light reaching the retina. In optical terms, the pupil is the eye's aperture, while the iris is the diaphragm. Eye color is defined by the iris."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Human_eye_close_up%2C_anterior_view.jpg/330px-Human_eye_close_up%2C_anterior_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iris_(anatomy)"},{"title":"Iris (plant)","blocks":[{"text":"Iris is a flowering plant genus of 320 accepted species with showy flowers. As well as being the scientific name, iris is also widely used as a common name for all Iris species, as well as some belonging to other closely related genera. A common name for some species is flags, while the plants of the subgenus Scorpiris are widely known as junos, particularly in horticulture. It is a popular garden flower."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iris_(plant)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Pupillary_light_reflex.jpg/500px-Pupillary_light_reflex.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Pupillary_light_reflex.jpg/1280px-Pupillary_light_reflex.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Pupillary_light_reflex.jpg/500px-Pupillary_light_reflex.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The iris (brown coloured portion of the eye) controls the size of the pupil by contracting the sphincter pupillae and dilator pupillae muscles.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APupillary_light_reflex.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Iris_regions.jpg/500px-Iris_regions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Iris_regions.jpg/1280px-Iris_regions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Iris_regions.jpg/500px-Iris_regions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIris_regions.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Auge_Iris.jpg/500px-Auge_Iris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Auge_Iris.jpg/1280px-Auge_Iris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Auge_Iris.jpg/500px-Auge_Iris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Light micrograph of the iris near to the pupil. M. sph. sphincter muscle, L lens","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAuge_Iris.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Doppler_holography_of_Olivier_Martinache%27s_myosis.gif/500px-Doppler_holography_of_Olivier_Martinache%27s_myosis.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Doppler_holography_of_Olivier_Martinache%27s_myosis.gif/500px-Doppler_holography_of_Olivier_Martinache%27s_myosis.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Constriction of the pupil (miosis) observed by laser Doppler imaging reveals radial vessels of the iris.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADoppler_holography_of_Olivier_Martinache's_myosis.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Voluntary_pupil_dilation.gif/500px-Voluntary_pupil_dilation.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Voluntary_pupil_dilation.gif/500px-Voluntary_pupil_dilation.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A human eye demonstrating its owner's rare ability to voluntarily dilate and constrict his pupil on command, via voluntary control of his iris muscles.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVoluntary_pupil_dilation.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/SD_OCT_-_Anterior_Chamber_Angle_Cross-Section_%28with_viewfinder%29.png/500px-SD_OCT_-_Anterior_Chamber_Angle_Cross-Section_%28with_viewfinder%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/SD_OCT_-_Anterior_Chamber_Angle_Cross-Section_%28with_viewfinder%29.png/500px-SD_OCT_-_Anterior_Chamber_Angle_Cross-Section_%28with_viewfinder%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Anterior chamber cross-section imaged by an SD-OCT.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASD_OCT_-_Anterior_Chamber_Angle_Cross-Section_(with_viewfinder).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Iris_structure.png/500px-Iris_structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Iris_structure.png/500px-Iris_structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Structure of the iris and surrounding parts showing the dilator and sphincter muscles (dilator pupillae and sphincter pupillae).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIris_structure.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Ageev_iris.jpg/500px-Ageev_iris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Ageev_iris.jpg/1280px-Ageev_iris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Ageev_iris.jpg/500px-Ageev_iris.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Among human phenotypes, blue-green-gray eyes are a relatively rare[citation needed] eye color and the exact color is often perceived to vary according to its surroundings.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAgeev_iris.jpg"}],"sounds":{"black":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8a/En-Black.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/8/8a/En-Black.ogg/En-Black.ogg.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/